// T4: a local declared inside a conditional block is reclaimed SAFELY.
//
// This probe began life as the negative case for condition 5, which refused such
// a local outright. codegen_arc_zero_slot retired that condition, so `inner`
// reclaims now and the probe gates the thing that makes it safe.
//
// THIS WAS A LIVE BUG, not a hypothetical. Measured on `main` before the fix,
// with `maybe(false)`:
//
//     Use of uninitialised value of size 8
//        at goo_release (runtime.c:203)      <- the immortal-count read
//        by maybe
//      Uninitialised value was created by a stack allocation at maybe
//
// and again at runtime.c:215, which is the __atomic_fetch_sub -- a WRITE through
// the garbage pointer, to an arbitrary address.
//
// WHY. codegen_alloc_local_promoted (src/codegen/function_codegen.c) sends an
// ordinary local to codegen_create_entry_alloca, so the SLOT is hoisted to the
// entry block. The initialising store stays at the DECLARATION SITE. A local
// declared inside a branch therefore has a slot on every path and a VALUE only on
// the taken one, and an LLVM alloca is undef rather than zero. goo_obj_headerless
// screens only NULL and goo_zerobase, so nothing downstream catches it.
//
// THE PROGRAM EXITED 0. The garbage on the stack happened to be benign, so a
// plain run proved nothing -- only valgrind saw it. That is why this probe is
// gated on valgrind's exit status and not on the program's.
//
// BOTH LOCALS ARE RELEASED NOW: `alive` at function scope and `inner` inside the
// branch. The 240,048 -> 0 differential is what proves the probe is not vacuous;
// before the zero store the ON run still leaked 24 bytes, because `inner` was
// refused. `alive` remains load-bearing -- without a function-scope local this
// probe would pass just as well with the release machinery switched off.
//
// NOTHING IS READ THROUGH A POINTER INTO A GLOBAL, and that is deliberate.
// escape_expr_taint's AST_UNARY_EXPR arm returns the operand's taint for EVERY
// unary operator -- right for `&x`, over-conservative for `*p`, where the deref
// copies the pointee out. Measured while writing this probe: `sink = sink + *alive`
// marked `alive` escaping, so it read RELEASE_NO_ESCAPES and NOTHING released.
// The probe was green and vacuous. examples/arc_release_probe.goo records the
// same arm as the reason it increments a counter, and this one does the same.
//
// The false path runs far more often than the true one, so the unwritten slot is
// the common case rather than a rare one.
//
// IMPORT-FREE, for the reason the other arc probes record.
package main

var sink int

func maybe(c bool) {
	alive := new(int)
	*alive = 1
	if c {
		inner := new(int)
		*inner = 2
		sink = sink + 1
	}
	sink = sink + 1
}

func main() {
	for i := 0; i < 10000; i++ {
		maybe(false)
	}
	maybe(true)
}
